    "Examinations were done by experienced sonographers who were not told the person's identity; images were stored, randomized and read blind by one sonographer and one independent observer, whose agreement on the FDA criteria was excellent (kappa above 0.9); the independent observer's data were used. Valve disease was defined by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) criteria for appetite-suppressant-induced valve disease: mitral regurgitation of moderate or greater severity (2/4 or more) or aortic regurgitation of mild or greater severity (1/4 or more); Fisher's exact test was used for the FDA score. Results: 33 people who took MDMA and 29 controls were selected, none with a history of cardiac disease; 4 people in the MDMA group were excluded after scanning (3 for inadequate image quality, 1 for mitral and tricuspid prolapse), leaving 29; all had used MDMA for at least 6 months. 8 people (28%) who took MDMA met the FDA criteria and none of the controls (p = 0.0045); mitral, aortic and tricuspid regurgitation and shape abnormalities were more often seen in the MDMA group (Table 1)."
